  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 700 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are expected this\nthis afternoon and evening, along with the potential for\nlocalized areas of flash flooding through tonight. \n\nA few showers have developed and will move across the FA, \nespecially eastern areas, between now and 10 am but the main \nshow comes later this morning into this afternoon and evening \nahead of a potent upper trough. Flow aloft strengthens \nconsiderably by the afternoon hours with 40-50 kt of flow noted \nat 500mb. Low level moisture also ramps up with PWAT values \nclimbing back above 2\". Instability is a bit more uncertain with\nclouds possibly hampering heating to some extent but models \nstill show 2000- 3000 J/kg of MLCAPE across the area. Storm mode\nis also somewhat uncertain with the potential for a mix of \nlinear segments and semi-discrete cells. All these factors point\nto a robust severe wx threat across the region. SPC has most of\nthe area in a Slight Risk (level 2/5) with a sliver of Enhanced\nRisk (lvl 3/5) clipping far northern portions of the MD Eastern\nShore. Could see the higher risk area being shifted a bit south\nand east in subsequent outlooks pending greater clarity on \ndegree of cloud cover and storm morphology. As is typical for \nMid-Atlantic July severe setups, the main threat will be from \ndamaging winds but a tornado or two cannot be completely ruled \nout, especially across the N and NE portions of the region where\nthe shear and low level helicity are progged to be strongest. \nThe threat of severe storms should last well into the evening \nhours if not into early Wednesday morning. Additionally, the \nfast storm motion will largely preclude a widespread flooding \nrisk. However, areas that get repeated storms late today into \ntonight due have a risk of flash flooding (SLGT ERO down to the \nVA/NC border). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The threat of severe thunderstorms will linger\ninto Wednesday and the potential for torrential rainfall to\nproduce scattered instances of flash flooding will also\nincrease through Wednesday night. \n\nThe severe threat likely continues into Wednesday, but with the\nhigher probabilities shifted to the south and east ahead of a \nsouthward-moving cold front. How convection evolves on Tuesday \nwill also influence the degree of cloud cover and \ndestabilization potential on Wednesday. That said, the flow \naloft is forecast to remain rather strong. SPC has a Slight Risk\noutlined for our entire CWA (lvl 2/5).\n\nDespite the ongoing drought conditions, Wednesday into Wednesday\nnight is showing an increasingly confident signal for localized\nflooding, especially in urban and poor drainage areas. WPC has \nmost of the area in a Slight Risk ERO again Wednesday with PWAT\ngenerally 2-2.25\". Decent flow aloft should keep individual \ncells moving but training storms on top of the rainfall received\nTuesday could cause scattered instances of flash flooding. All\ntotal, the QPF forecast through Thursday has generally 2-5\" of\nrain from Richmond and Emporia eastward to SE MD and NE NC. \n\nSome drier air finally moves back into the region Thursday and \nFriday but some showers and storms will remain possible, mainly \nacross the southern and southeastern portions of the area.",
